---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import OtpDemo from '../../components/OtpDemo.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import {reaRelease} from '../../data/rea-release';
const toc=[{id:'try',label:'Try the time boundary'},{id:'apk',label:'Locate the APK method'},{id:'trace',label:'Recover the calculation'},{id:'rebuild',label:'Rebuild and test'},{id:'scope',label:'What was actually verified'}];
---
<Guide eyebrow="Android / Worked case study" minutes="18 min" toc={toc}>
  <div class="lesson-summary"><strong>The goal</strong><p>Recover one time-based code calculation, reproduce it in the browser, and check the boundary between two 30-second windows.</p><strong>Before you start</strong><p>The demo works immediately. To repeat the APK inspection, you need REA, Aegis v3.4.3, a full JDK 17+ and the jadx provider described in our <a href="/tutorials/android-api/">Android guide</a>.</p></div>
  <h2 id="try">01. Move from 59 to 60 seconds</h2><p>Click the two time buttons below. With the fixed public test key, 59 seconds produces <code>287082</code>; 60 seconds produces <code>359152</code>. Move back to 30 seconds: the first code returns because 30 and 59 fall inside the same time window.</p><OtpDemo/>
  <p>These numbers use seconds since the Unix epoch, not the seconds displayed on your wall clock. The demonstration implements standard TOTP with the public ASCII key <code>12345678901234567890</code>. It does not read an Aegis vault or use an account secret.</p>
  <h2 id="apk">02. Find the time-based method in the APK</h2><p>The <a href="https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/website/evidence/aegis-login-code.md">official REA report</a> inspected Aegis v3.4.3 with REA 6.1.0. To follow its trail, <a href="https://github.com/beemdevelopment/Aegis/releases/tag/v3.4.3">obtain that release</a>, record its hash and search for the TOTP class. Commands below pin REA {reaRelease.version}. Their names and arguments were checked against that release’s CLI help on {reaRelease.lastVerifiedLabel}. This site did not re-run them against the APK.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={`npx -y rea-agents@${reaRelease.version} inspect-android-package "/absolute/path/aegis-v3.4.3.apk" --json\nnpx -y rea-agents@${reaRelease.version} search-android-classes "/absolute/path/aegis-v3.4.3.apk" Totp --json`}/>
  <p>Use the exact returned class name to inspect its members. The timestamp overload of <code>getOtp</code> is useful because you can supply the time directly. Select its overload from the inventory instead of assuming that the first method with that name is the right one.</p>
  <CodeBlock prompt label="Investigation prompt" code="Use REA to inspect the TotpInfo class in /absolute/path/aegis-v3.4.3.apk. Select the getOtp overload that accepts a timestamp. Follow its calls through counter encoding, HMAC, truncation and decimal formatting. Recover default period, hash and digit count. Cite each method inspected and distinguish defaults from per-account settings. Do not execute the APK."/>
  <h2 id="trace">03. Recover the whole calculation</h2><p>Finding a division by 30 is only the start. The upstream report connects five stages: floor time into a counter; encode that counter in eight big-endian bytes; compute HMAC with the key; select a positive 31-bit value from the hash; reduce it to six digits and retain leading zeroes.</p>
  <div class="flow"><span>Time</span><i>→</i><span>Counter</span><i>→</i><span>HMAC</span><i>→</i><span>6 digits</span></div>
  <p>Follow returned method calls rather than relying on familiar source names. In the inspected release, optimization placed generator helpers under <code>kotlin.ExceptionsKt</code>. The report also distinguishes default SHA-1, six digits and 30 seconds from account-specific settings.</p>
  <h2 id="rebuild">04. Rebuild the rule and test the edges</h2><p>Implement the recovered behavior independently, then use public reference vectors. Test just before and after a time boundary, a leading-zero result and both six- and eight-digit output. Adjacent windows are not mathematically guaranteed to have different codes, even though this example does.</p>
  <CodeBlock prompt label="Reconstruction prompt" code="Write a small independent TOTP implementation for this recovered behavior. Use only RFC 6238 public test keys. Verify all six SHA-1 reference timestamps at eight digits and their six-digit remainders. Test the 29/30 and 59/60 boundaries and preserve leading zeroes. Explain any difference from the inspected method; do not claim to rebuild the vault or Android app."/>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Unix time</th><th>Counter</th><th>6-digit result</th><th>What it checks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>29</td><td>0</td><td>755224</td><td>End of first window</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>1</td><td>287082</td><td>Boundary crossed</td></tr><tr><td>59</td><td>1</td><td>287082</td><td>Same window</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td>2</td><td>359152</td><td>Next counter</td></tr><tr><td>1111111109</td><td>37037036</td><td>081804</td><td>Leading zero retained</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <h2 id="scope">05. Know what “133 checks” means</h2><p>The upstream project reports agreement on 133 inputs between a JVM harness assembled from selected decompiled methods and independent reconstructions. It did not launch the original APK. That supports the selected calculation, not complete application equivalence.</p><p>For this site, we wrote a separate WebCrypto demonstration and checked the six published SHA-1 vectors at two digit lengths, plus every integer second from 0 to 120 against a separate Node HMAC implementation. We did not repeat the original APK investigation or its Java harness.</p>
  <div class="sources"><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/morluto/rea/blob/main/website/evidence/aegis-login-code.md">REA’s Aegis evidence report</a> · <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238#appendix-B">RFC 6238 test vectors</a>. Demo and explanation independently authored. No Aegis application source is redistributed here.</p></div>
